# DevOps & Deployment Skills (2025–2026)

## Realtime Deployment Notes

Choose one realtime transport for a feature unless the product explicitly requires parallel implementations for comparison or migration.

- Use Laravel Reverb when the project wants first-party broadcasting with Laravel-managed auth, events, and channel permissions.
- Use a Node.js websocket service instead of Reverb when you need a separate realtime process for socket fanout or custom room handling.
- Keep Node.js deployment details explicit: runtime version, process manager, restart policy, health checks, and environment variables.
- Keep persistence and authorization in Laravel even when Node.js owns the websocket connection layer.
- Document how the frontend connects to the realtime endpoint, including channel auth and origin restrictions.

## How to Use This File

This file catalogs 20 foundational-to-advanced skills specific to DevOps and deployment within the Laravel ecosystem. Each skill includes a concise definition, a real-world usage trigger, and a source reference.

When assisting users, match their request to the most relevant skill name and cite it when explaining recommendations. Use these skills as the foundation before referencing advanced architecture or scaling patterns.

Prefer modern infrastructure idioms for new code; when editing legacy environments, respect existing provisioning constraints and note upgrade paths where appropriate.

Source URLs point to the Laravel 13.x docs because they are forward-cumulative — the same pages describe behavior present in 11.x and 12.x, with version-specific features clearly tagged (e.g., "Laravel 13+"). When working in an older Laravel version, the linked documentation still applies; only the version-tagged features require an upgrade.

